{% extends 'shells/docs.html.twig' %}
{% block title %}Scopes · php-via Docs{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
<article>
    <h1>Scopes</h1>
    <p>
        A scope decides which tabs share a signal and which tabs a broadcast renders again. Every tab has a TAB
        scope of its own. Give a signal a shared scope, and state that was private becomes multiplayer.
    </p>

    <h2 id="scope-types">Scope types</h2>

    <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: var(--font-size-0); margin-block: var(--size-5);">
        <thead>
            <tr style="border-block-end: 2px solid var(--border);">
                <th style="text-align: left; padding: var(--size-2) var(--size-3); font-weight: var(--font-weight-7);">Scope</th>
                <th style="text-align: left; padding: var(--size-2) var(--size-3); font-weight: var(--font-weight-7);">Shared with</th>
                <th style="text-align: left; padding: var(--size-2) var(--size-3); font-weight: var(--font-weight-7);">Use case</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr style="border-block-end: 1px solid var(--border);">
                <td style="padding: var(--size-2) var(--size-3);"><code>Scope::TAB</code></td>
                <td style="padding: var(--size-2) var(--size-3);">Nobody (this tab only)</td>
                <td style="padding: var(--size-2) var(--size-3);">Personal UI state, form input</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border-block-end: 1px solid var(--border);">
                <td style="padding: var(--size-2) var(--size-3);"><code>Scope::ROUTE</code></td>
                <td style="padding: var(--size-2) var(--size-3);">Every tab on the same route</td>
                <td style="padding: var(--size-2) var(--size-3);">Live feeds, shared boards, todo lists</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border-block-end: 1px solid var(--border);">
                <td style="padding: var(--size-2) var(--size-3);"><code>Scope::SESSION</code></td>
                <td style="padding: var(--size-2) var(--size-3);">Every tab of one browser session</td>
                <td style="padding: var(--size-2) var(--size-3);">User preferences, auth state</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border-block-end: 1px solid var(--border);">
                <td style="padding: var(--size-2) var(--size-3);"><code>Scope::GLOBAL</code></td>
                <td style="padding: var(--size-2) var(--size-3);">Every tab, on every route</td>
                <td style="padding: var(--size-2) var(--size-3);">Site-wide banners, system notifications</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="padding: var(--size-2) var(--size-3);"><code>'room:lobby'</code></td>
                <td style="padding: var(--size-2) var(--size-3);">The tabs that joined this custom scope</td>
                <td style="padding: var(--size-2) var(--size-3);">Chat rooms, collaborative documents, teams</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
        Wherever a context takes a scope, in <code>scope()</code>, <code>addScope()</code>,
        <code>removeScope()</code>, <code>signal()</code> and <code>#[Action(scope: ...)]</code>,
        <code>Scope::ROUTE</code> becomes this route's scope and <code>Scope::SESSION</code> this session's. A
        route scope belongs to the route as registered: on <code>/blog/{slug}</code>, <code>Scope::ROUTE</code>
        is <code>Scope::routeScope('/blog/{slug}')</code>, shared by every post. For one scope per post, use a
        custom scope such as <code>'post:' . $slug</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="who-a-scope-reaches">Which tabs a scope reaches</h2>
    <p>
        A route scope reaches every tab on its route, and <code>Scope::GLOBAL</code> every tab. A session scope
        and a custom scope reach the tabs that joined them, which a context does in three ways:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>a signal declared with the scope joins it,</li>
        <li><code>$c-&gt;scope($scope)</code> makes it the context's primary scope,</li>
        <li><code>$c-&gt;addScope($scope)</code> adds it, and <code>$c-&gt;removeScope($scope)</code> leaves it again.</li>
    </ul>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->signal(0, 'online', 'room:lobby'); // joins room:lobby
$c->addScope('user:' . $userId);       // joins user:42 as well
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h2 id="setting-a-scope">The primary scope</h2>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
// The target of $c->broadcast() and the key of a shared render
$c->scope(Scope::ROUTE);
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        The primary scope does two things: <code>$c-&gt;broadcast()</code> renders the tabs in it, and a view
        with <code>shareRender: true</code> shares its render with them. It is no default for anything else.
        A signal without a scope stays private, so after <code>scope()</code> set a shared scope,
        <code>signal()</code> without one throws and names the fix. An action runs for the tab that posts it.
        <code>scope()</code> replaces the primary scope set before and keeps the scopes the context joined with
        <code>addScope()</code> or a scoped signal.
    </p>

    <h3 id="scope-and-the-view-cache">Scope and the shared render</h3>
    <p>
        Each tab renders its own update on a broadcast, which a view that shows anything client-specific (a
        username, a TAB signal's id, a personal viewport) needs:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->addScope($roomScope);

// Renders per tab: the view embeds this user's name and TAB signal ids.
$c->view('room.html.twig', fn (): array => [...]);
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        If the view really is identical for every tab, set the shared scope with
        <code>$c-&gt;scope($roomScope)</code> and pass <code>shareRender: true</code> to <code>view()</code>: the
        first context renders, and every context with the same primary scope, route and component gets the
        same HTML. See <a href="/docs/views#caching">Sharing one render</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="broadcasting">Broadcasting</h2>
    <p>
        A write to a scoped signal broadcasts its scope by itself. To render the tabs of a scope after a change
        outside signals, such as a database row or GlobalState, broadcast it:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->broadcast(Scope::routeScope('/board'));              // every tab on /board
$app->broadcast(Scope::sessionScope($c->getSessionId()));  // every tab of this session
$app->broadcast('room:lobby');                             // the tabs in a custom scope
$app->broadcast('room:*');                                 // every room
$app->broadcast(Scope::GLOBAL);                            // every tab
$c->broadcast();                                           // this context's primary scope
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        <code>$app-&gt;broadcast()</code> takes a resolved scope: the bare <code>Scope::TAB</code>,
        <code>Scope::ROUTE</code> and <code>Scope::SESSION</code> throw, since outside a context there is no route
        or session to resolve them against. <code>$c-&gt;broadcast()</code> on a context whose primary scope is TAB
        renders that tab only; reach the scopes it joined with <code>$app-&gt;broadcast()</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        A broadcast from an action or a timer is rendered once on the worker's next flush, however often it was
        called, and it reaches the other workers and, with a broker, the other servers.
        <code>$app-&gt;flushBroadcasts()</code> renders it at once. <code>Config::withBroadcastThrottle()</code>
        renders a busy scope at most once per interval, and the last state always arrives:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config->withBroadcastThrottle('import:*', 250); // each import renders at most 4 times a second
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>See <a href="/docs/broadcasting">Broadcasting</a> for the timing and for presence.</p>

    <div class="docs-demo">
        <div class="docs-demo-header">A TAB counter beside a ROUTE counter</div>
        <div class="docs-demo-body">
            <p style="font-size: var(--font-size-0); color: var(--text-3); margin-block-end: var(--size-4);">
                Open a second tab, then increment both counters. The TAB counter belongs to this tab; everyone on this page shares the ROUTE counter.
            </p>
            <div class="scope-split" id="scope-demo">
                {{ tabDemo | raw }}
                {{ routeDemo | raw }}
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="session-scopes">Session scopes</h2>
    <p>
        A session is one browser's session cookie, which all its tabs send. <code>$c-&gt;getSessionId()</code>
        returns the session's id, a hash of the cookie, and <code>Scope::sessionScope($id)</code> the scope of its
        tabs, which is what <code>Scope::SESSION</code> resolves to in a context. The cookie's value never
        reaches the page, the Dev Bar, traces or broker messages. Middleware reads the same id from the
        <code>via.session</code> request attribute.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>$c-&gt;regenerateSession()</code> gives the session a new cookie at a login and keeps its id, so its
        scope, its SESSION signals and its tabs stay as they are. See
        <a href="/docs/actions#login">Logging in</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="counting">Counting the tabs in a scope</h2>
    <p>
        <code>$app-&gt;countClients($scope)</code> counts the tabs with an open stream that a broadcast of the scope
        reaches, on every worker. Use it to skip work nobody watches:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->setInterval(function () use ($app, $prices): void {
    if ($app->countClients(Scope::routeScope('/ticker')) === 0) {
        return; // nobody is watching
    }
    $app->setGlobalState('price', $prices->latest());
    $app->broadcast(Scope::routeScope('/ticker'));
}, 1000);
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        A tab counts from its SSE connect until its stream closes. <code>$app-&gt;getLocalContexts($scope)</code>
        lists this worker's contexts in a scope, connected or not, and <code>$app-&gt;getClients()</code> lists
        every connected tab.
    </p>

    <h2 id="custom-scopes">Custom scopes</h2>
    <p>Any other string is a custom scope. Tabs that join the same string share its signals and receive its broadcasts:</p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->page('/chat/{room}', function (Context $c, string $room): void {
    $roomScope = 'room:' . $room;
    $messages  = $c->signal([], 'messages', $roomScope); // joins the room
    $draft     = $c->signal('', 'draft');

    $c->action(function () use ($messages, $draft): void {
        $messages->mutate(fn (array $list): array => [...$list, $draft->string()]);
        $draft->setValue('');
    }, 'send');

    // ... view ...
});
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        A pattern with <code>*</code> reaches every matching scope in a broadcast, so <code>'room:*'</code>
        renders every room. With more than one worker or server, a broadcast crosses to the others only for a
        scope made of letters, digits and <code>_ - . : /</code>, so build custom scopes from keys you control.
        See the <a href="/examples">chat room example</a> for a full implementation.
    </p>

    <h2 id="scope-lifetime">When a scope's signals go</h2>
    <p>
        A worker drops a scope's signals and actions when its last context in that scope is destroyed. With one
        worker, the next tab that declares such a signal starts from its initial value again. Keep a value that
        has to outlive its visitors in GlobalState or your database, and seed the signal from it:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$visits = $c->signal($app->globalState('visits', 0), 'visits', Scope::ROUTE);
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h2 id="actions-and-scopes">Actions and scopes</h2>
    <p>
        <code>$c-&gt;action()</code> takes no scope: an action runs for the tab that posts it, whatever scopes the
        tab is in. A class page can declare <code>#[Action(scope: ...)]</code> to register one action for a
        whole scope, which still runs on the posting tab's own instance. See
        <a href="/docs/composition#actions">Composition</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="next-steps">Next steps</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="/docs/signals#scopes">Signals</a>: declaring a shared signal</li>
        <li><a href="/docs/broadcasting">Broadcasting</a>: timing, presence and GlobalState</li>
        <li><a href="/docs/views#caching">Views</a>: sharing one render in a scope</li>
        <li><a href="/docs/api#scope">API: Scope</a>: the constants and helpers</li>
    </ul>
</article>

{% endblock %}
